116	// reconcilePrompt is the static system prompt for the reconcile pass. The
117	// dynamic candidate/existing lists are supplied on stdin (see reconcileInput).
118	// Like the distill prompt it must not begin with a dash — the agent runners
119	// pass it as a positional CLI argument.
120	func reconcilePrompt() string {
121		return `You are deduplicating durable repository facts. On stdin you receive two
122	numbered lists: CANDIDATES (new facts just extracted from a session) and
123	EXISTING (facts already stored at the same taxonomy paths). For each candidate,
124	decide how it relates to the existing facts.
125	
126	For every candidate output exactly one line, in candidate-number order:
127	
128	    <candidate#> <decision> <existing#-or-dash> <confidence>
129	
130	- <candidate#>: the candidate's number.
131	- <decision>: one of
132	    new        — not represented by any existing fact.
133	    merge       — the SAME meaning as an existing fact (a restatement); consolidate.
134	    supersede   — CONTRADICTS or replaces an existing fact.
135	- <existing#-or-dash>: for merge/supersede, the EXISTING fact number it refers
136	  to; for new, a dash "-".
137	- <confidence>: 0.00 to 1.00, your confidence in a merge/supersede decision
138	  (use 1.0 for new).
139	
140	Rules:
141	- Candidates are newly extracted, not necessarily newer evidence. Session processing may be newest-first; do not infer chronology from which list contains a fact.
142	- A restatement ("use tabs") and a reversal ("use spaces") look similar but are
143	  NOT the same: the first is merge, the second is supersede. Read the meaning.
144	- When unsure between merge/supersede and new, prefer new with lower confidence
145	  rather than wrongly collapsing distinct facts.
146	- Output only these lines. No prose, no headers, no blank lines.
147	
148	Example:
149	1 new - 1.0
150	2 merge 3 0.88
151	3 supersede 1 0.93`
152	}
